wifi_db: disable the 6GHz radio only, under HWSIM_RADIO.

Every 6GHz channel mac80211_hwsim offers is flagged no-IR ("no initiate
radiation") by the regulatory domain the host runs, so a 6GHz VAP can never
beacon in this environment. The host's global regdomain is country US, whose
6GHz rule reads

    (5925 - 7125 @ 320), (N/A, 12), (N/A), NO-OUTDOOR, PASSIVE-SCAN

and cfg80211 turns PASSIVE-SCAN into IEEE80211_CHAN_NO_IR. All 59 of the phy's
6GHz channels carry it; none are disabled.

Verified directly rather than inferred, by attempting an AP start on a free
hwsim radio with an identical wpa_supplicant configuration at three
frequencies, changing nothing but the frequency:

    5180 MHz  (5GHz, no flags)      -> AP-ENABLED
    5865 MHz  (5GHz, no IR)         -> nl80211: Failed to set channel: -22
    5955 MHz  (6GHz ch 1, no IR)    -> nl80211: Failed to set channel: -22

The determinant is the no-IR flag, not the band -- a 5GHz channel carrying it
fails in exactly the same way, with the same errno, at the same point.

hwsim itself is not the limitation. It advertises the 6GHz band with all its
channels present and enabled, and it does advertise HE ("HE Iftypes: AP",
"HE Iftypes: managed", with full HE MAC and PHY capability sets) for both AP
and managed interface types. An earlier version of this patch claimed 6GHz
failed because hwsim exposes no HE capability and 6GHz mandates HE. That was
wrong -- it came from a grep whose negative result was never sanity-checked.
The radios are perfectly capable; they are simply not permitted to transmit.

2.4GHz and 5GHz both work, and both are kept enabled. They can only be UP
simultaneously if the module was loaded with channels>=2: hwsim defaults to
"#channels <= 1" (see "valid interface combinations"), and bringing up a
second band's VAPs while another band is active then fails with kernel error
-16 (EBUSY) on the first "Set freq". meta-lxd loads the pool with channels=2
for exactly this reason.

2.4GHz in particular has to stay enabled because the EasyMesh controller maps
the fronthaul haul onto its 2.4GHz radio. With that radio disabled the
controller kept the image default SSID while the extender served private_ssid,
so the two nodes never shared an ESS and client roaming could not be tested at
all.

Leaving the 6GHz radio enabled is not harmless: its em_t never reaches
em_state_agent_onewifi_bssconfig_ind, and unified-wifi-mesh's Topology Query
handler waits for every radio to get there before answering. See
unified-wifi-mesh patch 0003, which stops that handler blocking on a radio
that is disabled. Both changes are needed -- this one so the radio is honestly
marked disabled, that one so the disabled state is respected.

Real BananaPi R4 hardware is unaffected: all bands still default enabled there,
and a regulatory setup that actually permits 6GHz AP operation will bring the
radio up normally.

Capability gate (review P1 #2): the no-IR constraint above is specific to the
6.8-era regulatory domain. On a 7.0 host loaded with regtest=5 the hwsim custom
domain (custom_03) leaves 6GHz IR-capable and a 6GHz AP beacons on 5975 -- proven
standalone in doc/easymesh/Linux-7.0-hwsim-6GHz-VLP-AP-results.md. So the disable
must not be unconditional under HWSIM_RADIO; it is gated additionally on
!defined(HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE). Building with -DHWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE (set
HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE = "1" in the recipe, off by default) keeps 6GHz enabled so a
capable host can exercise wifi2. NOTE: enabling 6GHz also needs the security
defaults to become band-aware (6GHz mandates SAE-H2E + PMF; patches 0005/0007
force WPA2) -- see review P1 #3 -- so -DHWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE alone is necessary but
not sufficient for a standards-correct tri-band VAP.

---
 source/db/wifi_db.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/source/db/wifi_db.c b/source/db/wifi_db.c
--- a/source/db/wifi_db.c
+++ b/source/db/wifi_db.c
@@ -98,6 +98,34 @@
     }
 
     cfg.enable = true;
+#if defined(HWSIM_RADIO) && !defined(HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE)
+    /* Disable the 6GHz radio: every 6GHz channel mac80211_hwsim offers is
+     * flagged no-IR by this host's regulatory domain, so a 6GHz VAP can never
+     * beacon here. Confirmed by attempting an AP start on a free hwsim radio
+     * with one configuration at three frequencies:
+     *
+     *     5180 MHz (5GHz, no flags)   -> AP-ENABLED
+     *     5865 MHz (5GHz, no IR)      -> Failed to set channel: -22
+     *     5955 MHz (6GHz, no IR)      -> Failed to set channel: -22
+     *
+     * The no-IR flag is what decides it, not the band. hwsim advertises the
+     * 6GHz band and does advertise HE for AP and managed iftypes; the radios
+     * are capable, just not permitted to transmit.
+     *
+     * 2.4GHz and 5GHz both work and both stay enabled. They can only be UP at
+     * the same time if the module was loaded with channels>=2 -- hwsim
+     * defaults to "#channels <= 1" and the second band's first "Set freq"
+     * then fails with -16 (EBUSY). meta-lxd loads the pool with channels=2.
+     *
+     * 2.4GHz must stay enabled because the EasyMesh controller maps the
+     * fronthaul haul onto its 2.4GHz radio; disabling it left the controller
+     * on the image default SSID while the extender served private_ssid, so the
+     * two nodes never shared an ESS. Real BananaPi R4 hardware is unaffected. */
+    if (cfg.band != WIFI_FREQUENCY_5_BAND && cfg.band != WIFI_FREQUENCY_5L_BAND &&
+        cfg.band != WIFI_FREQUENCY_5H_BAND && cfg.band != WIFI_FREQUENCY_2_4_BAND) {
+        cfg.enable = false;
+    }
+#endif /* HWSIM_RADIO && !HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE */
 
     switch (cfg.band) {
         case WIFI_FREQUENCY_2_4_BAND:
